USER MANUAL
Creating and activating a python virtual environment and installing a package

What this procedure achieves
By the end, you will have an isolated Python workspace (a virtual environment) for one project, so that packages you install for it do not affect other projects or your computer's main Python installation.

Prerequisites
Before starting, make sure you have all of the following:
1.	A computer running Windows 10/11, macOS, or Linux.
2.	Python 3.8 or newer installed. To check, complete Step 2 below. If Python is missing, download it from python.org. On Windows, tick "Add Python to PATH" during installation.
3.	An internet connection, needed to download the package.
4.	A terminal application: 
•	Windows: Command Prompt or PowerShell (search for either in the Start menu)
•	macOS: Terminal (in Applications → Utilities)
•	Linux: your distribution's Terminal
5.	About 50 MB of free disk space.
6.	Basic terminal knowledge: you should know how to type a command and press Enter. No other experience is needed.
Note on commands: Lines starting with $ or > show what you type. Do not type the $ or > symbol itself. Where Windows and macOS/Linux differ, both versions are given.

Procedure
Step 1: Open your terminal
Open the terminal application listed in the prerequisites for your operating system.
Expected result: A window opens showing a text prompt, such as C:\Users\YourName> (Windows) or yourname@computer ~ % (macOS/Linux), with a blinking cursor.

Step 2: Check that Python is installed
Type the following and press Enter:
•	Windows: python --version
•	macOS/Linux: python3 --version
Expected result: The terminal prints a version number such as Python 3.12.1. Any version of 3.8 or higher is acceptable. (If you see "command not found" or "not recognized", see the Troubleshooting note.)
Remember your command: Throughout this guide, use python if that worked for you in this step, or python3 if that is the one that worked.

Step 3: Create a project folder
Type the following and press Enter:
mkdir my_project
Expected result: The terminal returns to a new empty prompt with no error message. A folder named my_project now exists in your current location.

Step 4: Move into the project folder
Type the following and press Enter:
cd my_project
Expected result: The prompt changes to include my_project (for example, C:\Users\YourName\my_project>), showing you are now working inside that folder.

Step 5: Create the virtual environment
Type the following and press Enter:
•	Windows: python -m venv venv
•	macOS/Linux: python3 -m venv venv
This tells Python to create a virtual environment in a new folder called venv.
Expected result: The terminal pauses for a few seconds, then returns to the prompt with no message. This silence means success. A new folder named venv now exists inside my_project.

Step 6: Confirm the venv folder exists
Type the following and press Enter:
•	Windows: dir
•	macOS/Linux: ls
Expected result: The listing includes an entry named venv.

Step 7: Activate the virtual environment
Type the command for your system and press Enter:
•	Windows (Command Prompt): venv\Scripts\activate
•	Windows (PowerShell): venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
•	macOS/Linux: source venv/bin/activate
Expected result: The prompt now begins with (venv), for example (venv) C:\Users\YourName\my_project>.
