Step 8: Check which Python the environment is using
Type the following and press Enter:
•	Windows: where python
•	macOS/Linux: which python
Expected result: The first path printed contains my_project and venv, for example C:\Users\YourName\my_project\venv\Scripts\python.exe. This proves you are using the isolated Python, not the system-wide one.

Step 9: Install the requests package
Type the following and press Enter:
pip install requests
Expected result: The terminal prints download and installation messages and ends with a line similar to Successfully installed requests-2.31.0 (the numbers may differ, and other packages may be listed alongside it).

Step 10: Verify the package was installed
Type the following and press Enter:
pip list
Expected result: A table of installed packages appears, and requests is one of the rows.

Step 11: Test the package in Python
Type the following and press Enter to start Python's interactive mode:
python
Expected result: The prompt changes to >>>, and a line showing the Python version appears above it.

Step 12: Import the package
At the >>> prompt, type the following and press Enter:
import requests
Expected result: The prompt returns to >>> with no error message. Silence means the package loaded correctly. (If you see ModuleNotFoundError, see the Troubleshooting note.)

Step 13: Exit Python
Type the following and press Enter:
exit()
Expected result: The prompt returns to (venv) ...> (your normal terminal prompt), showing you have left Python's interactive mode but are still inside the virtual environment.

Step 14: Deactivate the virtual environment
When you finish working, type the following and press Enter:
deactivate
Expected result: The (venv) prefix disappears from the prompt, showing you are back in your normal system environment.

Troubleshooting note: the most common beginner error
Problem: ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'requests' (or pip installs the package but Python cannot find it)
Why it happens: This is almost always caused by forgetting to activate the virtual environment, or by opening a new terminal window and not re-activating it. Activation lasts only for the terminal window where you ran it. Without (venv) in the prompt, pip install puts the package into your system Python, while the code runs somewhere else, or the reverse.
How to fix it:
1.	Look at your prompt. If (venv) is missing, the environment is not active.
2.	Navigate to your project folder: cd my_project
3.	Re-run the activation command from Step 7.
4.	Confirm (venv) appears at the start of the prompt.
5.	Run pip install requests again, then retest.
Related issue (Windows PowerShell only): If Step 7 shows "running scripts is disabled on this system," run this once, then retry activation:
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned
Alternatively, use Command Prompt instead of PowerShell and run venv\Scripts\activate.
Golden rule: Before installing or running anything, check that (venv) is in your prompt.

Quick reference summary
Task	Windows	macOS/Linux
Create environment	python -m venv venv	python3 -m venv venv
Activate	venv\Scripts\activate	source venv/bin/activate
Install package	pip install requests	pip install requests
Deactivate	deactivate	deactivate

Note on the "one action per step" rule: Steps 6, 8, 10, and 12 are verification steps, each containing a single command whose output confirms the previous step worked. Keeping checks as separate steps helps beginners find exactly where something went wrong.

